Vulnerability Title
Lack of Root Detection (Application Runs on Rooted Android Devices)

OWASP Category
M7:2024 – Insufficient Binary Protections (aligned with MASVS-RESILIENCE)

CVSS 3.1 Vector
CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:N

Description
The Android mobile application lacks effective root detection mechanisms, allowing it to be installed and executed without restriction on a rooted Android device. On a rooted device, the standard Android application sandbox is broken, granting superuser (root) privileges to users and other installed applications. Without runtime integrity and root checks, the application operates in an untrusted environment where its local data, memory, and execution flow can be easily inspected or manipulated.

Impact
Bypass of Android Sandbox & Local Data Exposure: Malicious apps or an attacker with physical/ADB access on a rooted device can read the application's private internal storage directory (/data/data/<package_name>/), exposing cached files, SQLite databases, or SharedPreferences.

Runtime Manipulation & Hooking: Root privileges allow attackers to attach dynamic instrumentation frameworks (such as Frida or Xposed) to hook functions, bypass client-side security controls (e.g., SSL pinning, biometric prompts), and dump sensitive data from memory.

Increased Risk for Sensitive Workflows: For financial or authentication applications, running on a compromised OS significantly lowers the barrier for reverse engineering, credential theft, and session hijacking.

Proof of Concept (PoC) Steps & Captions
Step 1: Verify that the test Android device has active root privileges (e.g., via Magisk, SuperSU, or by running adb shell su and confirming the # root prompt).

PoC 1 Caption: Terminal/device output confirming active root (su) access on the Android test device.

Step 2: Install the target Android application APK onto the rooted device and launch it.

PoC 2 Caption: Application successfully installed and launched on the rooted Android device without any root-detection warning or termination.

Step 3: Navigate through the application's core screens (such as login or dashboard) to confirm full functionality is permitted in the rooted environment.

PoC 3 Caption: Application functioning normally on the rooted device, confirming the absence of runtime root detection controls.

Mitigation
Implement Multi-Layered Root Detection: Combine multiple detection checks rather than relying on a single indicator—such as checking for su binaries in system paths, inspecting Build.TAGS for test-keys, detecting known root management packages/daemons, and checking for writable system partitions.

Use Hardware-Backed Attestation: Integrate the Google Play Integrity API to cryptographically verify device integrity on the backend server before allowing sensitive operations or logins.

Enforce Native & Obfuscated Checks: Implement root detection logic in native C/C++ (JNI) alongside Java/Kotlin, obfuscate the code, and vary checks to make static patching and Frida hooking harder.

Define a Risk-Based Response: When root is detected, either block execution with a clear security warning or enforce server-side policy restrictions (e.g., disabling high-risk transactions) based on the application's threat model.

References
